From 1b5859c8901f8388146b23b7a664780253d917a9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 18:48:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] chore(hygiene): CRAWL-02 - the crawl report is a measurement,
 not a source file
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`node scripts/crawl-site.mjs` left three untracked files behind:

    ?? audit/site-crawl.csv
    ?? audit/site-crawl.json
    ?? audit/site-crawl.md

The rule for this already existed and this one was simply missed when it was
written. .gitignore has carried, for a while:

    # audit-rendered writes a report per run, against whatever host it was
    # pointed at. Committing it means the repo carries one person's measurement
    # of one deployment as if it were a fact about the code.
    audit/rendered.json
    audit/rendered.md

crawl-site.mjs is the same thing exactly: it fetches every route from a LIVE
host and writes down what came back. Two people crawling on the same afternoon
would produce different files and neither would be wrong, and a file like that
sitting in the tree invites somebody to read it as the state of the site rather
than as the state of one request at one moment. The report that matters is the
exit code.

So the three join the two, under the same reasoning, written out rather than
appended silently — the next person to add a script that writes a report should
be able to see why their output belongs here too.

Not deleted from history: they were never committed. `git check-ignore` confirms
all three are matched, and `git status` on audit/ is clean with the files
present on disk.

VERIFIED

  65 of 66 guards pass; verify:css fails only in this sandbox for want of
  node_modules.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Wz3f94TUzSk49wD2izgQYx
---
 .gitignore | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/.gitignore b/.gitignore
index 79175e3..68385c4 100644
--- a/.gitignore
+++ b/.gitignore
@@ -92,6 +92,16 @@ package-lock.json
 audit/rendered.json
 audit/rendered.md
 
+# crawl-site.mjs is the same thing and was missed when that rule was written:
+# it fetches every route from a LIVE host and writes what it found, so the file
+# is a snapshot of a deployment at a moment, not a property of this repository.
+# Two people crawling on the same day would commit different files and neither
+# would be wrong. Run it, read it, throw it away — the report that matters is
+# the exit code.
+audit/site-crawl.json
+audit/site-crawl.csv
+audit/site-crawl.md
+
